From: Andre Eikmeyer <dev@deq.rocks>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/pm: let Apple Navi boards lower the power limit to 40 % of TDP

The pptables of the Navi GPUs in Apple Macs carry no overdrive power limit
range, so the power limit can only be set to exactly the TDP. The Mac SMC
shares one heatpipe between CPU and GPU and asks the GPU driver for lower
power limits when it runs out of budget. The macOS driver builds these
steps from the TDP down to 40 % of it.

Allow the same range on Apple boards when the pptable has no lower bound.

Tested on a MacBookPro16,1 (Navi 14, 50 W TDP) under combined CPU and GPU
load, with a user space daemon that applies the SMC steps. The power cap
range is now 20 to 50 W. When the shared heatpipe ran over its target, the
SMC lowered the dGPU zone target from 70 to 45 W and asked for dGPU steps 2
to 5. The cap followed at 46, 44, 42 and 40 W, and the SMC handed the freed
budget back to the CPU, from 1300 to 2200 MHz. Without this change the cap
stays at 50 W and the CPU alone has to absorb the heatpipe limit.

Signed-off-by: Andre Eikmeyer <dev@deq.rocks>
---

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/navi10_ppt.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/navi10_ppt.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/navi10_ppt.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/navi10_ppt.c
@@ -2232,6 +2232,14 @@ static int navi10_init_ppt_limits(struct
 			SMU_11_0_ODSETTING_POWERPERCENTAGE]);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Apple boards have no overdrive power limit range. The macOS driver
+	 * builds its power limit steps from the TDP down to 40 % of it, and the
+	 * Mac SMC uses them to share the heatpipe between CPU and GPU.
+	 */
+	if (!od_percent_lower && smu->adev->pdev->subsystem_vendor == PCI_VENDOR_ID_APPLE)
+		od_percent_lower = 60;
+
 	for (i = SMU_POWER_SOURCE_AC; i < SMU_POWER_SOURCE_COUNT; i++) {
 		smu->ppt_limits.range[i][SMU_PPT_LIMIT_PPT0].default_value =
 			default_limit[i];
